<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cornelius Vanderbilt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1794">1794</a>–<a href="/years/1877">1877</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cornelius Vanderbilt was an American shipping and railway <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a>, one of the richest men of the nineteenth century. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> Gold Rush his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accessory-transit-company">Accessory Transit Company</a> carried travellers between the Atlantic and Pacific across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> from 1851, and in 1856–1857 he helped the Central American states drive out the filibuster <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">William Walker</a> after Walker seized the company's property. He later built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> Central Railroad.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cornelius-vanderbilt.jpg" alt="Cornelius Vanderbilt" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Produced by Mathew Brady's studio, restored by Michel Vuijlsteke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cornelius_Vanderbilt_Daguerrotype2_(cropped)(b).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vanderbilt was born on Staten Island on 27 May 1794 and left school at eleven to work on his father's ferry in New York Harbor. At sixteen he began his own ferry to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a>, and other captains called him the Commodore in jest, a nickname that stuck. In 1817 he began working for Thomas Gibbons, who was fighting a steamboat monopoly granted by the New York legislature. In Gibbons v. Ogden the Supreme Court ruled for Gibbons on 2 March 1824, holding that states could not interfere with interstate commerce.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1829 Vanderbilt worked for himself and built steamboat lines around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-island-sound">Long Island Sound</a> and on the Hudson, where in 1834 he competed as The People's Line until the monopoly paid him to stop. By the end of the 1830s he dominated steamboats on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">the Sound</a> and began to take over connecting railroads. With the gold rush he turned to ocean steamships and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accessory-transit-company">Accessory Transit Company</a> to carry passengers across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>. During the Civil War he gave his largest steamship, the Vanderbilt, to the Union Navy and received a Congressional Gold Medal.</p><p>In 1863 he took control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and Harlem Railroad through a stock corner, and in 1864 he sold his last ships to concentrate on railroads. He bought the Hudson River Railroad in 1864 and the New York Central in 1867, and in 1870 merged two lines into the New York Central and Hudson River Railroad, one of the first giant corporations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. His attempt to corner Erie Railway stock led to the Erie War with Jay Gould and James Fisk Jr.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1869 he married Frank Armstrong Crawford, and at her urging gave one million dollars, then the largest charitable gift in American history, to found Vanderbilt University in Nashville. He died on 4 January 1877, aged 82, with an estimated fortune of 105 million dollars, 95 per cent of it left to his son William and four grandsons. Two daughters and a son challenged the will in court, and William settled. His biographer T. J. Stiles credits him with expanding the nation's transport infrastructure, while critics call him a robber baron for monopolies helped in part by political manipulation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cornelius Vanderbilt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the California Gold Rush <b>Cornelius Vanderbilt</b>’s Accessory Transit Company carried thousands of travellers across the isthmus by steamboat and stagecoach from 1851, and a canal through Nicaragua remained a dream of investors and governments for more than a century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Staten Island, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.57,-74.15&amp;year=1847" aria-label="Staten Island on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Cornelius Vanderbilt"><image href="/maps/globes/g300n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4090 4045h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4090 4045h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.57,-74.15&amp;year=1847">What was here in 1847? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
